DP-05 | DPA | The customer gives general written authorisation for sub-processors. Bramber maintains a current list in the DPA, notifies changes at least 30 days in advance, and gives the customer a right to object on reasonable data protection grounds. Bramber remains liable for its sub-processors' performance.
The sub-processor list in the DPA shall state, for each sub-processor:
its legal name and registered address;
the processing it performs and the categories of personal data concerned;
the country of processing and the transfer mechanism, where applicable. | Prior written consent for new sub-processors, not unreasonably withheld or delayed, with deemed approval after 10 Business Days and a right to use affiliates without consent. | Absolute consent for each sub-processor, a veto over cloud infrastructure providers, or a requirement that Bramber's affiliates be treated as third parties requiring consent. | Elena Marchetti, DPO. Tom Whitlock, Senior Commercial Counsel for any consent right beyond general authorisation. | Our platform runs on hyperscale infrastructure and a small number of specialist services. A veto right over sub-processors is a veto over our ability to operate and scale. General authorisation with notice and objection is the model the EDPB guidelines contemplate for stable processing chains.

